%% CANARY for rule 1, the highest-signal finding of the three: a direction line mermaid throws away.
%% Both subgraphs declare direction LR and the author reasonably believes they get two short rows.
%% They do not: the edge a2 --> b1 joins a node INSIDE one box to a node INSIDE the other, so
%% mermaid discards BOTH direction lines and falls back to the parent TB. Both boxes are marked
%% below, because one crossing edge breaks the direction at both ends of it.
%% Measured on this exact pair of shapes: joined container-to-container 397x284, joined node to
%% node 142x689. Same nodes, same labels, one edge moved.
%% EXPECT: ignored-direction | blind DIRECTION_RE — with no direction line recognised there is
%% nothing being discarded, so the rule must go silent. That is what proves this finding is about
%% the DECLARATION and not merely about an edge crossing a boundary.
flowchart TB
%% MUST-FIRE
  subgraph A["left box"]
    direction LR
    a1["one"] --> a2["two"]
  end
%% MUST-FIRE
  subgraph B["right box"]
    direction LR
    b1["three"] --> b2["four"]
  end
  a2 --> b1
